# Upstream: giac 2.1.0 usual.cc ln and sqrt: ln(inv(f)) returns -ln(f) and sqrt(inv(f)) returns inv(sqrt(f)) for any f, identities that fail on the negative real axis, where they give the conjugate of the principal value: ln(1/(1-sqrt(2))) = -ln(1-sqrt(2)), evalf 0.88137358702-3.14159265359*i (right 0.88137358702+3.14159265359*i), sqrt(1/(pi-4)) = 1/sqrt(pi-4), evalf -1.07932761087*i (right +1.07932761087*i); also sec and csc of an angle where cos or sin is negative (sqrt(sec(2)) = 1/sqrt(cos(2)), ln(sec(2)) = -ln(cos(2))), log10 through ln, and ln of the fraction 1/-2 that inv(-2) and a subst give (ln(inv(-2)) = -ln(-2)). A free name was rewritten at once, so a value put in later took the conjugate: ln(1/y) = -ln(y), and subst(ln(1/y),y=-2) evalf -0.69314718056-3.14159265359*i (right +3.14159265359*i); sqrt(1/y) = 1/sqrt(y), at y = -2 -0.707106781187*i (right +0.707106781187*i). Now the rule runs only where sign(f) is 1 (a positive constant, x^2+1, exp(x), a name under assume(y>0)), and for a fraction 1/d with d > 0 in ln; elsewhere ln and sqrt keep inv(f) and evalf gives the principal value (ln(1/y) stays ln(1/y), sqrt(1/y) stays sqrt(1/y); ln(1/2) = -ln(2) as before).
